[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Online validation with OCSP (/docs/platform/pki/ocsp)\n\n\n\n<span id=\"ocsp-service\" />\n\nOCSP queries a certificate’s status and returns a signed response. This guide is for an integrator checking what Lamassu publishes and how the consumer must verify it. For complete identity validation, follow [Certificate validation](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation).\n\nHow the responder works in Lamassu [#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu]\n\nThe responder retrieves the certificate by serial number and locates its issuing CA in the inventory. It translates persisted status as follows:\n\n| Record status                                    | Signed response                                           |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ACTIVE`                                         | `Good`.                                                   |\n| `REVOKED`                                        | `Revoked`, with revocation reason and timestamp.          |\n| Other states, including `INACTIVE` and `EXPIRED` | `Unknown` if the certificate and issuer can be retrieved. |\n\nAn absent serial number causes a lookup error; it does not guarantee a signed `Unknown` response. Lookup or signing errors produce an HTTP failure. Treat that as unavailable evidence according to consumer policy.\n\nThe issuing CA itself signs the response through its KMS-associated key. A delegated OCSP signing certificate is not used. An imported CA without a usable key cannot sign responses.\n\nThe implementation sets `thisUpdate` approximately one day before response generation and `nextUpdate` one day after: the declared window is about 48 hours. Inspect received values and apply your freshness policy. The response reflects stored status when queried; a previously cached copy does not change upon revocation.\n\nEmbedding in issued certificates [#embedding-in-issued-certificates]\n\nThe signer constructs OCSP URLs from configured VA domains and adds them to AIA. Without domains, there is no URL to add; an imported certificate retains its original extensions. Inspect the actual certificate:\n\n```bash\nopenssl x509 -in device.crt -noout -ocsp_uri\n```\n\nCheck that the consumer can reach this URL. DNS, endpoint exposure and domain configuration belong to [Deployment](/docs/deployment/self-hosted/networking). Changing configuration does not modify already signed certificates.\n\nHTTP endpoints [#http-endpoints]\n\nThe service accepts:\n\n| Method | VA service path        | Format                                                 |\n| ------ | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `POST` | `/ocsp`                | DER request, `Content-Type: application/ocsp-request`. |\n| `GET`  | `/ocsp/{ocsp_request}` | DER request encoded using padded URL Base64.           |\n\nUse POST to avoid client encoding differences. A successful response has `Content-Type: application/ocsp-response` and a DER body. The public prefix depends on the Gateway; use the URL published in the certificate and checked by the operator.\n\nGenerate and verify a query [#generate-and-verify-a-query]\n\nYou need OpenSSL 3.x, `device.crt`, its direct issuer’s certificate `issuer.pem` and the consumer’s trusted roots in `roots.pem`. For an intermediate issuer, also prepare `intermediates.pem` containing the intermediate CAs.\n\nGenerate the request:\n\n```bash\nopenssl ocsp -issuer issuer.pem -cert device.crt \\\n  -no_nonce -reqout request.der\n```\n\nThis example omits the nonce because the responder does not echo it. Verification relies on signature, response correspondence and timestamps; it does not demonstrate freshness through a nonce.\n\nReplace the example URL with the inspected endpoint:\n\n```bash\nOCSP_URL='http://pki.example.com/ocsp'\n\ncurl --fail --show-error --silent --max-time 15 \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/ocsp-request' \\\n  --data-binary @request.der \"$OCSP_URL\" \\\n  -o response.der\n```\n\nVerify the returned DER:\n\n```bash\nopenssl ocsp -respin response.der \\\n  -issuer issuer.pem -cert device.crt \\\n  -CAfile roots.pem -no_nonce -resp_text\n```\n\nFor intermediates, add `-verify_other intermediates.pem`. Require successful response verification and inspect the status corresponding to `device.crt`, issuer, serial, `thisUpdate` and `nextUpdate`. `Response verify OK` validates the response only: also observe `good`, `revoked` or `unknown`. Do not accept an identity based only on the command’s exit code.\n\nCheck from the interface [#check-from-the-interface]\n\n**OCSP Check** shows what Lamassu returns for a selected certificate. Complete the check from the consumer’s network and trust store: a console query does not establish its validation configuration.\n\nTo check a change, save an earlier response, revoke a test certificate and request another response. The new query should reflect persisted status; compare timestamps to rule out reuse of an earlier response. If it fails, follow [PKI troubleshooting](/docs/platform/pki/troubleshooting).\n\nReferences [#references]\n\n* [RFC 6960: OCSP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6960).\n* [OpenSSL: openssl-ocsp](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-ocsp/).\n\n<Cards>\n  <Card title=\"CRL service\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/crl\" />\n\n  <Card title=\"Certificate validation\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation\" />\n</Cards>\n"]
